### Describe the feature you'd like to request

It would be nice if tRPC had a built in feature to handle permissions checks. We could have an additional `useGuard` hook that would receive the same inputs as the underlying procedure, but would just run until all of the `guard middlewares` have finished running.

### Describe the solution you'd like to see

As opposed to the alternate solution described below, I think this would give a better DX:

### Backend

```typescript
router({
    example: protectedProcedure
        // we are using guard instead of middleware for permission checks
        .guard(async (opts) => {
            const permissions = await getPermissions(opts.ctx.user);

            if(!permissions.includes("EXAMPLE_PERMISSION")) {
                throw new TRPCError({
                    message: "You don't have permission to do this!",
                    code: "FORBIDDEN",
                });
            }

            return opts.next();
        })
        .mutation(...),
});    
```

### Frontend

```typescript
const ExampleComponent = () => {
    const { hasPermisison, error } = trpc.example.useGuard();
    const { mutate, isLoading } = trpc.example.useMutation();

    return (
        <Button onClick={mutate} loading={isLoading} disabled={!hasPermisison}>
                {error.message ?? "Click me!"}
        </Button>
    );
};
```

The `useGuard` hook could work just as a regular `useQuery`, and would return a boolean indicating that the guards have passed all of the checks (or would return undefined until the server don't respond). If an error occured during permission checks, then we would just return the error as if it was a regular query.

### Describe alternate solutions

Currently, we are using middlewares to handle permission checks like this:

### Backend

```typescript
router({
    example: protectedProcedure
        .use(async (opts) => {
            const permissions = await getPermissions(opts.ctx.user);
            if(!permissions.includes("EXAMPLE_PERMISSION")) {
                throw new TRPCError({
                    message: "You don't have permission to do this!",
                    code: "FORBIDDEN",
                });
            }

            return opts.next();
        })
        .mutation(...),

    permission: router({
        list: protectedProcedure
                  .query((opts) => getPermissions(opts.ctx.user)),
    }),
});    
```

### Frontend

```typescript
const ExampleComponent = () => {
    const [permissions] = trpc.permission.list.useSuspenseQuery();
    const { mutate, isLoading } = trpc.example.useMutation();

    if(!permissions.includes("EXAMPLE_PERMISSION")) {
        return "You don't have permission to do this!";
    }

    return (
        <Button onClick={mutate} loading={isLoading}>
                Click me!
        </Button>
    );
};
```

As you can see, this works, but we have to write a permission checks both on frontend and backend. It becomes a problem, when we edit the backend and not the frontend (or vice versa).

